  <h1>What to Do After Wrongful Termination: A Step-by-Step Action Plan</h1>

  <p>
    The hours and days after a termination are disorienting. But if you believe your firing was illegal, the steps you take immediately — and in the weeks that follow — can make or break a potential legal claim. Evidence gets lost. Access to company systems disappears. Deadlines run. Here is a practical action plan.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1: Document Everything Before You Leave</h2>
  <p>
    If you have any opportunity before your company access is terminated, preserve the records that are relevant to your claim. This means:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Performance reviews and commendations:</strong> Copies of any written performance evaluations showing satisfactory or strong performance</li>
    <li><strong>Communications about the termination:</strong> Any emails, messages, or notes about the decision, the reasons given, or events leading up to it</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence of protected activity:</strong> Records showing you made a complaint to HR, requested FMLA, filed a workers' comp claim, or engaged in any other protected activity</li>
    <li><strong>Your offer letter and employment contract:</strong> The original terms of your employment</li>
    <li><strong>The employee handbook:</strong> Especially policies on progressive discipline, termination procedures, and investigations</li>
    <li><strong>Any communications showing discriminatory or retaliatory motive:</strong> Messages, emails, or notes containing relevant remarks by supervisors or HR</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Important: Only preserve information you have legitimate access to as an employee. Do not access systems after your employment ends, and do not take confidential business information unrelated to your own employment situation. Overreach in document preservation can create legal problems for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: Write Down Everything You Remember</h2>
  <p>
    Within 24–48 hours of your termination, write a detailed chronological account of events: what was said at the termination meeting, who was present, what reasons were given, the sequence of events leading up to the firing, any prior incidents you believe were related, and any statements by supervisors that might be relevant. Memory fades quickly, and a contemporaneous written account can be powerful evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Include dates, names, exact quotes where you can remember them, and descriptions of the setting and context. Keep this document somewhere secure and separate from your work devices.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3: Understand Your Filing Deadlines — They Are Short</h2>
  <p>
    If you believe you have a federal discrimination or retaliation claim, you must file a charge with the EEOC before you can sue. The deadline is:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>180 days</strong> from the discriminatory act in states without a state fair employment agency</li>
    <li><strong>300 days</strong> from the discriminatory act in states with their own agency (most states)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    State law claims have varying deadlines — some are as short as 1 year, some as long as 3 years. FMLA claims must be filed within 2 years (3 for willful violations). Missing any of these deadlines typically bars your claim permanently. If you're not sure whether a deadline applies to you, consult an attorney immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4: Consult an Employment Attorney as Soon as Possible</h2>
  <p>
    Most employment attorneys who represent wrongful termination plaintiffs offer free initial consultations and work on contingency — meaning no upfront cost to you. An attorney can: assess whether your facts support a viable claim; identify all applicable statutes and their deadlines; advise on EEOC filing; evaluate any severance agreement you've been offered; and give you a realistic sense of what your case might be worth.
  </p>
  <p>
    You should consult an attorney even if you're not sure you have a claim. The consultation is free, and the downside of not consulting — missing a filing deadline, signing away your rights in a severance agreement — can be severe.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 5: File an EEOC Charge if Required</h2>
  <p>
    For federal discrimination and retaliation claims, filing an EEOC charge is a mandatory prerequisite to suing in court. You can file online at publicportal.eeoc.gov, by mail, or in person at an EEOC field office. An attorney can help you draft a charge that accurately describes your situation and preserves all potentially applicable legal theories.
  </p>
  <p>
    Filing a charge does not commit you to litigation — most charges lead to mediation offers or conciliation attempts before the EEOC issues a Right to Sue letter. It does, however, start the administrative clock and protects your ability to sue if the process doesn't resolve your claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 6: Start Actively Looking for New Work — and Document It</h2>
  <p>
    You have a legal obligation to mitigate damages by making reasonable, good-faith efforts to find comparable employment. Start your job search immediately and keep a detailed log: every application, every recruiter contact, every interview, every rejection. Note the date, company, position, salary range, and outcome.
  </p>
  <p>
    This log has two purposes: it demonstrates your good-faith mitigation efforts (protecting your back pay claim), and it documents your difficulty finding comparable work if you need to support a front pay claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 7: Do Not Sign Any Severance Agreement Without Legal Review</h2>
  <p>
    If your employer offered severance in exchange for a release of claims, do not sign anything until an attorney has reviewed it. Severance agreements routinely waive all employment claims — discrimination, retaliation, FMLA, contract, everything — in exchange for a few weeks or months of pay. The value of a potential wrongful termination claim often far exceeds the offered severance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Employees over 40 have specific rights under the Older Workers Benefit Protection Act (OWBPA): you must be given at least 21 days to consider the agreement (45 days in a group layoff), and you have 7 days to revoke after signing. Your employer cannot shorten these periods.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 8: Take Care of Yourself</h2>
  <p>
    This is practical as well as personal. Emotional distress damages in a wrongful termination case require evidence of real harm — and courts look at whether you sought mental health treatment, took medication for anxiety or depression, experienced relationship or family difficulties, or had documented physical symptoms from the stress of the termination. If the experience has genuinely affected your mental health, seeking treatment both addresses your wellbeing and creates medical records that support your damages claim.
  </p>

